{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NVA5yPxmvVNpxW9CSzdKZj","type":"tool_result","content":"skills/prisma-8/SKILL.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/debug.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/migrations.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/upgrade-app.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/upgrade-extension.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/migration-review.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/quickstart.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/contract.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/supabase.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/build.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/migration-model.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-mongo.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/runtime.md\nskills/prisma-8/references/feedback.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/0.15-to-0.16/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.2-to-8.0.0-rc.3/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/app/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.9-to-8.0.0-rc.10/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.8-to-8.0.0-rc.9/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.3-to-8.0.0-rc.4/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.10-to-0.11/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.15-to-8.0.0-rc.16/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.9-to-0.10/stamp-storage-types-kind.ts\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.9-to-0.10/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.22-to-8.0.0-rc.23/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.8-to-0.9/strip-inline-contracts.ts\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/0.8-to-0.9/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.4-to-8.0.0-rc.5/instructions.md\nskills/prisma-8/upgrading/extension/upgrades/8.0.0-rc.16-to-8.0.0-rc.17/instructions.md\n---\nskills/prisma-8/SKILL.md:69:| Write queries | [`references/queries.md`](references/queries.md) | query, where, select, project, orderBy, limit, offset, take, skip, include, lookup, first, all, count, aggregate, groupBy, create, update, delete, upsert, returning, transaction, `db.orm`, `db.sql`, `runtime.query(plan)` vs `runtime.execute(plan)`, `db.prepare` / prepared statements, streaming / `for await`, many-to-many `include` and nested `connect`, `and` / `or` / `not` from `@prisma/orm-postgres/orm-client`, `db.query.from(...)` (Mongo pipeline), namespace-aware accessors, `.all()` Thenable, single-use iterators (`RUNTIME.ITERATOR_CONSUMED`), target-declared aggregate types (`count`, integer `sum`, and integer `avg` are `number`; `count` and integer `sum` throw outside ±(2^53 − 1) rather than round, while `avg` is a fraction already and carries no guard; `countBigInt` / `sumBigInt` / `avgDecimal` are the lossless forms, `avgDecimal` on PostgreSQL only), custom collection class (`extends Collection`), `with` and query fragments (`QueryFragment`, `Filtered`, `Ordered`), shared query fragments and scopes (`db.orm.fragment(fields, body)` for a filter on every model with given fields, `CodecField`, `db.orm.public.Post.fragment(...)` for a shared `select` / `include`, `orderByField` for an order or sort parameter from a request), drizzle-style, kysely-style. Naming types (§ *Naming model and result types*): \"type of my model\", \"return type of a query\", `ResultType`, `Scalars`, `Shape`, `models` / `Models` namespace in `contract.d.ts`, `Models.public_User`, Prisma 7 `Prisma.User` / `GetPayload` / `UserGetPayload` equivalents. Postgres/SQLite specifics: [`references/queries-postgres.md`](references/queries-postgres.md); Mongo specifics: [`references/queries-mongo.md`](references/queries-mongo.md) |\nskills/prisma-8/references/migrations.md:480:   - if it inserts rows the application needs, insert them by hand.\nskills/prisma-8/references/debug.md:104:| `DRIVER.NOT_CONNECTED` with message `Runtime is closed` | A query, prepared statement or `db.runtime().connection()` that starts after `db.close()` and after the runtime was idle for one turn of the event loop, or after the `await using` scope that held a serverless connection ended | The work started after the runtime closed. Almost always it is a lazy read (`db.orm...all()`, `db.query(...)`) returned without `await`, which starts only when the caller awaits it, or work that waited on something other than the database first. Write `return await ...`, or move the call before `close()`. See `references/runtime.md` § *Workflow — Serverless and per-request runtimes*. |\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:52:Critical to get right early — on **both Postgres and Mongo**, `.all()` returns an **`AsyncIterableResult<Row>`**, which is *both* a `PromiseLike<Row[]>` and an `AsyncIterable<Row>`. That means three consumption forms all work, and the canonical one is the shortest:\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:55:const users = await db.orm.public.User.select('id', 'email').all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:69:// here, use `await ...all()` and skip `.toArray()`.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:70:const rows: Promise<User[]> = db.orm.public.User.select('id', 'email').all().toArray();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:74:for await (const user of db.orm.public.User.select('id', 'email').all()) {\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:82:const user = await db.orm.public.User.where({ id }).all().first();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:84:const required = await db.orm.public.User.where({ id }).all().firstOrThrow();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:88:For genuine single-row reads, prefer the *collection*-level `.first()` or `.firstOrThrow()` (which add `LIMIT 1` to the SQL on Postgres) over `.all().first()` / `.all().firstOrThrow()` (which fetch all rows and discard the rest). Collection-level `.firstOrThrow()` runs the same query as `.first()`, returns the row without `null`, and rejects with `RUNTIME.NO_ROWS` when nothing matches: `await db.orm.public.User.firstOrThrow({ id })` on Postgres, `await db.orm.users.where({ email }).firstOrThrow()` on Mongo (no arguments there). The result-level helpers are for cases where you already need the full result and want the first row without an extra round-trip.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:94:const result = db.orm.public.User.select('id', 'email').all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:99:const users = await db.orm.public.User.select('id', 'email').all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:104:If you've seen `collect(...)` / `toArray(...)` helpers in a codebase wrapping `.all()`, they're vestigial — `await` does the same thing for free. Remove them when you touch the surrounding code.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:116:  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:121://   await db.orm.users.create(u);\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:130:A plan from `db.sql`, `db.raw` or the ORM (`.prepared.all().plan`) runs on the client: `db.query(plan)` returns its rows as an `AsyncIterableResult`, and `db.execute(plan)` resolves `{ affectedRows }` with no rows. Inside a transaction use `tx.query` / `tx.execute`; they have the same types. On Supabase, call them on a role-bound client (`db.asAnon()`, `await db.asUser(jwt)`). `db.query` on an ORM plan yields the database rows, not the result the ORM builds from them. To run a plan, use `db.query` and `db.execute`, not `db.runtime()`. `db.runtime()` remains for runtime-level work: `connection()`, `close()`, `telemetry()`, and as the target of a prepared statement (`ps.query(db.runtime(), params)`).\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:137:const plan = db.orm.public.Post.where({ published: true }).prepared.all().plan;\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:143:Every read terminal (`.all()`, and `db.query(plan)` for a SQL-builder plan) returns an `AsyncIterableResult`, so `for await` is always available. What it buys you depends on the `cursor` option, which `postgres()` and `postgresServerless()` (`@prisma/orm-postgres/serverless`) both accept:\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:146:- **`cursor` set in the options of `postgres()` or `postgresServerless()` (`{ batchSize: 100 }`; `{}` or `{ batchSize: undefined }` for batches of 100; a `batchSize` that is not a positive integer fails the factory call; there is no flag that turns cursors off):** the driver reads through a server-side cursor in batches of that size, so `for await` over `db.orm...all()` or `db.query(plan)` really does stream, and an early `break` stops reading. In a Worker, put the option only on a second serverless client that the streaming paths open their connections from, and keep the serverless client every other path uses without it. On a connection with cursors on, finish or `break` the `for await` loop before sending another query through `db`: the cursor holds the connection's only database connection until the loop ends, so a query inside the loop waits forever. On a client, the same query takes another database connection from the pool. Behind Cloudflare Hyperdrive, reads with cursors on hang, so the streaming paths hang there and the other paths do not.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:172:The model is the whole row plus its relations, and each related model carries its own relations in turn, so no query returns a value of the model type. A query returns the fields it fetched. The default fetch returns `Scalars<Model>`, the model without relations: `db.orm.public.User.first()` returns `Scalars<Model> | null`, and `db.orm.public.User.all()` returns `Scalars<Model>[]` (or its async iterable). Four types cover every case, and none needs a client in scope:\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:177:- `ResultType<typeof query>` — the row of any ORM collection value (plain, `.include()`, `.select()`, `.variant()`), and of SQL lane plans. Bind the query to a name first; `typeof` needs a value. `.variant()` takes the variant's discriminator value, not its model name: `db.orm.public.Task.variant('bug')` for `@@base(Task, \"bug\")`. Call it once, on the base collection; a second `.variant()` on a variant collection is refused.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:187:const usersWithTasks = () => db.orm.public.User.include('tasks');\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:198:    .include('posts', (posts) => posts.include('tags'))\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:220:2. **Writing a `collect()` / `toArray()` helper to convert `.all()` to an array.** `.all()` returns an `AsyncIterableResult<Row>` which *is* a `PromiseLike<Row[]>` — `await collection.all()` directly yields `Row[]`. See *Consuming the result* above.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:227:- **Many-to-many relations work through the junction.** `.include('tags', (tag) => tag.select(...))` traverses an N:M relation's `through` table, and nested `create` / `connect` / `disconnect` on an N:M relation write the junction rows for you (`examples/prisma-8-demo/src/orm-client/get-post-tags.ts`, `create-post-with-tags.ts`). The one refusal: a junction with required payload columns the relation API cannot populate throws `ORM.RELATION_MUTATION_UNSUPPORTED` — write that junction directly or use the SQL builder.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:236:- **Automatic N+1 detection.** Prisma 8 does not warn when an `.include(...)` is missing. Workaround: be deliberate about `.include(...)` in code review; the `lints` middleware (see `references/runtime.md`) catches the more common authoring slips (missing `WHERE` on a `DELETE` / `UPDATE`, missing `LIMIT` on a `SELECT`).\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:250:- [ ] Used `.first()` / `.first({ pk })` (Postgres) or `.where({ ... }).first()` (Mongo) for single-row reads — not `.all()`.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries.md:251:- [ ] Consumed `.all()` with plain `await` (not a `collect()` / `toArray()` helper). Used `for await` only when per-row handling is actually wanted — and did not promise it bounds memory on the long-lived façade — and never iterated the same result twice.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:11:- **`db.orm.<ns>.<Model>`** — ORM, PascalCase model name (`db.orm.public.User`). Fluent `.where(...).select(...).orderBy(...).all()`, fully typed against `Contract`. Default lane for CRUD with relations.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:21:| Eager-load related records | **ORM `.include(...)`** | Composes with `.where` / `.select` / `.orderBy` / `.limit` per branch. |\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:23:| `INSERT ... RETURNING` / `UPDATE ... RETURNING` typed result | **ORM mutations** (returns updated rows) or **`db.sql.<ns>.<t>.insert(...).returning(...)`** | ORM returns inserted/updated rows; SQL builder exposes `.returning(...)` explicitly. |\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:30:The concept: `db.orm.<ns>.<Model>` returns a *collection* you compose method-by-method. Each call returns a new collection (immutable chaining); the terminal verb (`.all()` / `.first()` / `.aggregate(...)`) issues the query. There is no `.count()` terminal on the collection — `count()` is an `include` reducer and an `aggregate(...)` operation (both below). Predicates are lambdas over a field proxy: `u.field.<op>(value)`.\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:50:  .all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:85:  .all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:91:  .all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:130:const posts = await db.orm.public.Post.select('id', 'title').using.post_search(q).limit(20).all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:131:const mine = await db.orm.public.Post.where({ userId }).using.post_search(q).all();\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:133:  .include('posts', (posts) => posts.using.post_search(q).limit(3))\nskills/prisma-8/references/queries-postgres.md:134:  .all();","is_error":false}]},"p