{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DRfp2r5NF53tUT4Nh9ccyL","type":"tool_result","content":"## Running queries from a short script\n\nWhen the user is running a one-off `tsx my-script.ts` (not a long-lived server), call `await db.close()` at the end so the process exits cleanly — on Postgres the façade-owned pool keeps Node's event loop alive; on Mongo the façade-owned `MongoClient` does the same. See `references/runtime.md` § *Running as a script (teardown)* for the full pattern including `await using`.\n\n```typescript\n// src/scripts/seed.ts\nimport { db } from '../prisma/db';\n\n// Postgres — PascalCase model root from contract\nfor (const u of users) {\n  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\n}\n\n// Mongo — lowercased plural root from contract (e.g. users, not User)\n// for (const u of users) {\n//   await db.orm.users.create(u);\n// }\n\nconsole.log('Seeded.');\n-----\nquickstart.md:8:- **First-touch orientation** — the user has arrived at a Prisma 8 project for the first time (a scaffold tool like `npx createprisma` dropped them in, they cloned a teammate's repo, or they ran `prisma orm init` themselves and now want to make their first move) and they're asking *\"what can I do with Prisma 8?\"*, *\"where do I start?\"*, or *\"what's next?\"*. The goal is to anchor them on the contract, get them connected to a database, round-trip one row, and let further commands surface organically.\nquickstart.md:17:- User just ran `createprisma` (or equivalent scaffold tool) and is asking what to do next. **First-touch orientation** path.\nquickstart.md:57:    └── app/                          ← created on first `migration plan` / `db init`\nquickstart.md:88:await db.orm.public.User.create({ email: 'alice@example.com' });\nquickstart.md:101:> **Mongo target:** the snippet above is SQL-target shape. On `@internal/mongo`, `db.orm` is keyed by the collection's storage name (`@@map(...)`, or the lowercased model name if no `@@map`), so the same arc reads `await db.orm.users.create(...)` / `await db.orm.users.select('id', 'email').all()` — not `db.orm.public.User`. Full rule and rewrite recipe in `references/queries.md` § *MongoDB ORM addressing*.\nquickstart.md:115:Triggers: *\"what can I do with Prisma 8?\"*, *\"what can I do next with Prisma?\"*, *\"where do I start?\"*, *\"I just ran createprisma\"*, *\"what's next?\"*, or any close variant — paired with a PN project already on disk (scaffolded by `createprisma`, by `prisma orm init`, by a teammate, however).\nquickstart.md:222:The concept: against an existing database with no PN contract, `contract infer` walks the live schema (tables, columns, indexes — including expression and partial ones — constraints, and RLS enablement + policies) and writes a PSL contract that describes it. Where authoring would generate a CHECK constraint the database does not carry (the element-non-null check on a list column), infer writes the list with nullable elements (`String?[]`), which derives no such check, so the contract declares exactly what the database enforces. The reverse gap is closed too: a hand-written CHECK constraint the database enforces that authoring would never have generated comes back as `@@check(expression: <reprint>, map: \"<name>\")`, so it is a declared object from the first pull instead of an invisible extra a later destructive plan could drop. That inferred check warns (`PN_EXACT_NAME_BODY_COMPARISON`) the next time you run `contract emit` — expected, not a defect: the warning fires on any `map:` body regardless of who wrote it, and the comparison stays sound because both sides are Postgres's own reprint. Columns come back as the convenience types with their defaults, and only the arguments that differ: `int2` as `Int(storage: int2)`, `numeric(10,2)` as `Decimal(precision: 10, scale: 2)`, `varchar(12)` as `String(storage: varchar, length: 12)`, `timestamptz(3)` as `DateTime` (a JavaScript `Date`), a `timestamptz` with no declared precision as `DateTime(precision: 6)`, `timestamp(3)` as `DateTime(storage: timestamp)`, `date` as `Date`, `time` as `Time` and `interval` as `Duration` (ISO strings). A `Date` holds milliseconds, so a timestamp column whose values use microseconds reads with them dropped: change such a field to `DateTime(as: temporal)` or `DateTime(as: string)` to keep them. The result is a *starting point*, not the final contract — review and clean it up, then `db sign` to record the current contract hash as the marker (instead of letting `db init` try to recreate the schema from scratch). By default `db sign` also sets the `db` ref and stores the contract snapshot, so the next `migration plan` chains from the adopted schema — even when the database is named with `--db`. `--advance-ref <name>` writes another ref instead and `--no-advance-ref` writes none; in either case the next plan needs `--from` or a `db` ref you set yourself.\nquickstart.md:305:- [ ] **All paths:** ran the first arc — one `create` + one `select` against the starter (or inferred) model — and got the round-trip working green.\nqueries-postgres.md:47:  .select('id', 'email', 'createdAt')\nqueries-postgres.md:48:  .orderBy((u) => u.createdAt.desc())\nqueries-postgres.md:133:  .include('posts', (posts) => posts.using.post_search(q).limit(3))\nqueries-postgres.md:208:  .orderBy([(p) => p.createdAt.desc(), (p) => p.id.desc()])\nqueries-postgres.md:217:  .orderBy((p) => p.createdAt.desc())\nqueries-postgres.md:223:  .orderBy((p) => p.createdAt.desc())\nqueries-postgres.md:224:  .cursor({ createdAt: last.createdAt })\nqueries-postgres.md:262:  .include('posts', (post) =>\nqueries-postgres.md:264:      .select('id', 'title', 'createdAt')\nqueries-postgres.md:265:      .orderBy((p) => p.createdAt.desc())\nqueries-postgres.md:270:// → Array<{ id, email, posts: Array<{ id, title, createdAt }> }>\nqueries-postgres.md:276:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.count()).all();\nqueries-postgres.md:279:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.sum('views')).all();\nqueries-postgres.md:282:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.avg('views')).all();\nqueries-postgres.md:285:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.min('views')).all();\nqueries-postgres.md:286:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.max('views')).all();\nqueries-postgres.md:290:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) => posts.sumBigInt('views')).all();\nqueries-postgres.md:294:await db.orm.public.User.include('posts', (posts) =>\nqueries-postgres.md:308:const user = await db.orm.public.User.create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nqueries-postgres.md:313:  .create({ id, email, displayName, kind, createdAt });\nqueries-postgres.md:327:// Upsert — typed by the create branch's shape.\nqueries-postgres.md:329:  .select('id', 'email', 'kind', 'createdAt')","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"42cf7c92-b21d-497e-9588-9df8d5da4efc","uuid":"73f49b60-e101-49b9-bb28-03dd8616c580","timestamp":"2026-11-10T03:08:26.582Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Running queries from a short script\n\nWhen the user is running a one-off `tsx my-script.ts` (not a long-lived server), call `await db.close()` at the end so the process exits cleanly — on Postgres the façade-owned pool keeps Node's event loop alive; on Mongo the façade-owned `MongoClient` does the same. See `references/runtime.md` § *Running as a script (teardown)* for the full pattern including `await using`.\n\n```typescript\n// src/scripts/seed.ts\nimport { db } from '../prisma/db';\n\n// Postgres — PascalCase model root from contract\nfor (const u of users) {\n  await db.orm.public.User.create(u);\n}\n\n// Mongo — lowercased plural root from contract (e.g. users, not User)\n// for (const u of users) {\n//   await db.orm.users.create(u);\n// }\n\nconsole.log('Seeded.');\n-----\nquickstart.md:8:- **First-touch orientation** — the user has arrived at a Pris